<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ratzeburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ratzeburg is a town on an island in the Ratzeburg Lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> founded a bishopric there in 1154, and its Romanesque brick cathedral survives. The town belonged to the dukes of Saxe-Lauenburg, while the bishopric's lands became the Principality of Ratzeburg under Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ratzeburg.jpg" alt="Ratzeburg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C.J.N.L. Kyll · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ratzeburger_Dom_im_Sommer.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ratzeburg is surrounded by four lakes, and the isthmuses between them form the access lanes to the town. It is the capital of the district of Herzogtum Lauenburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>. The town was founded in the 11th century as Racisburg, a name traditionally derived from the local Wendish ruler, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raciborz">Ratibor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabians</a>, who was nicknamed Ratse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1044 Christian missionaries led by the monk Ansverus came to the region and built a monastery, which a pagan rebellion destroyed in 1066; the monks were stoned to death. Ansverus was canonised in the 12th century and his relics were entombed in the cathedral. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> became ruler of the town in 1143 and founded a bishopric in 1154. He also had the late Romanesque cathedral built in the north German red-brick style. From 1180 part of the diocese formed a prince-bishopric, the last state in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to remain Catholic, which turned Lutheran in 1554.</p><p>The town itself lay outside the prince-bishopric, in the old Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and became part of Saxe-Lauenburg around 1296, staying with that duchy until 1876. In 1619 the capital of Saxe-Lauenburg moved to Ratzeburg from Lauenburg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. In 1693 Christian V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> bombarded the town into rubble in an unsuccessful attempt to win the dukedom from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>; it was rebuilt in the baroque style, but the castle was never rebuilt. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> of 1864 the estates offered the dukedom to the Prussian Hohenzollern dynasty, and on 1 July 1876 the duchy merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>'s Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Romanesque cathedral survives, and monuments in two of the town's churches commemorate the missionaries of 1066. From 1945 to 1989 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a> ran just east of the town, which stood on the border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. The sculptor Ernst Barlach made his home in Ratzeburg and is buried in one of its cemeteries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ratzeburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Ascanian dukes of Saxony divided their lands, the younger line received the scattered northern territories around Lauenburg on the Elbe and <b>Ratzeburg</b>, formally separated from the Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg in 1296.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg">Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>It remained rural, poor and sparsely populated, with large estates, and included the detached district of <b>Ratzeburg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Free State of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry the Lion founded the bishopric of <b>Ratzeburg</b> in 1154 during the conquest and conversion of the Slavic Polabians, with Evermod as its first bishop.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-ratzeburg">Prince-Bishopric of Ratzeburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bishopric of <b>Ratzeburg</b>, founded in the 12th century under Henry the Lion, had become Lutheran in the Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-ratzeburg">Principality of Ratzeburg</a></li></ul></section>
